#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:152 ../../source/units-identity.rst:296 ../../source/units-identity.rst:331 ../../source/units-identity.rst:446 ../../source/units-identity.rst:531 ../../source/units-identity.rst:565 ../../source/units-identity.rst:599 ../../source/units-identity.rst:633 ../../source/units-identity.rst:735 ../../source/units-identity.rst:769 ../../source/units-identity.rst:804
msgid "This attribute is optional."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:162
msgid "``Come back to this to determine date for claim``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:167
msgid "Researchers may use this field to make temporary notes or leave temporary comments intended for others in the research team about a claim. These should eventually be addressed and the field cleared by the researcher or research team. If the claim needs an explanatory note or comment to be better understood, then that should be entered in the :ref:`public_notes:meta <unit-public-notes-meta>` field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:171
msgid "citation:refs:claim"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:176
msgid "A unique 32 character code assigned to citation(s) evidencing the claim."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:181 ../../source/units-identity.rst:250
msgid "String in UUID format"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:196
msgid "``69dba35b-2b70-47cf-bfda-f80225f652c6``, ``4e99308c-f9c0-49e8-b97b-14c1e7bcb99d;bedf57b2-c20b-41e3-9dcf-b7b065eaa3b7``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:201
msgid "Every claim must have at least one citation to evidence the data in the claim. When two or more citations are needed to evidence a claim then a corresponding explanatory note should be entered in the :ref:`public_notes:meta <unit-public-notes-meta>` field. This field is for the Universally Unique Identifier (UUID) for each citation, found in the :ref:`source:access_point_id:admin` field in the Sources sheet. When multiple citations are needed every UUID should be semi-colon separated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:205
msgid "external_id"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:210
msgid "A number, UUID or other form of identification given to the unit by an external party."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:215 ../../source/units-identity.rst:291 ../../source/units-identity.rst:326 ../../source/units-identity.rst:405
msgid "Text string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:220
msgid "This is a draft field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:235
msgid "This field is intended to capture any external id given to a unit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:240
msgid "about_entity:ref:claim"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:245
msgid "A unique 32 character code assigned to each entity in the dataset."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:265
msgid "``521ebf18-f161-4ac9-8c72-5a246efa0458``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:270
msgid "Every entity has a Universally Unique Identifier (UUID) to distinguish it from any other entity. For a :ref:`unit` this UUID distinguishes them from any other :ref:`unit` in the dataset. This UUID is used in other fields to tie a :ref:`unit` to a :ref:`positioning`, :ref:`relation`, :ref:`posting`, or :ref:`incident`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:272
msgid "If a unit undergoes a change in name, that unit should be treated as a separate, distinct unit and given its own UUID."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:277
msgid "A citation establishes that a :ref:`unit`, named the ``Tiger Forces`` in Syria was renamed the ``25 Anti-Terrorism Division`` in 2019. The division continued to serve with the same general functions as before, and continued to be commanded by the same person. However, because a citation clearly evidenced that there had been a change in the name of the :ref:`unit`, the ``25 Anti-Terrorism Division`` should be treated as a distinct :ref:`unit` with a separate ``about_entity:ref:claim`` from the ``Tiger Forces``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:281
msgid "about_entity:name:qa"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:286
msgid "Field that provides human readable name for the claim."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:301
msgid "``n/a``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:306
msgid "``510 Light Infantry Battalion``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:311
msgid "This field provides a human readable counterpart to the :ref:`about_entity:ref:claim <unit-about-entity>`. This field can be manually added by a researcher or automatically populated by the system after import. For a :ref:`unit` best practice is to use the :ref:`name:annotation <unit-name-annotation>` in this field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:316
msgid "name:annotation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:321
msgid "Fullest name of the unit as evidenced by citations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:341
msgid "``3 Armoured Division``, ``3 Compañía de Infantería No Encuadrada``, ``7 Military Operations Command``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:346
msgid "The Security Force Monitor strives to create a single canonical version of a unit's name that is based on citations, highlights differences between peer units, and makes the data easier for a general audience to understand. Similar to a :ref:`person`, wherever possible we will choose the most complex version of a unit’s name that can be evidenced by a citation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:350
msgid "There are 18 citations which reference a unit's name as ``3 Division`` and only two citations which call it ``3 Armoured Division``. Even though there are many more citations for ``3 Division``, the :ref:`name:annotation <unit-name-annotation>` should be ``3 Armoured Division``. The shorter ``3 Division`` would only be used in :ref:`unit:names:assertion`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:352
msgid "Units have several unique characteristics that require additional and unit specific methodological guidance. Units in security forces are arranged in hierarchies and share similar names with their peer units. However, while citations may establish general guidance for how units of a certain type should be named, each individual unit may not have citations evidencing the \"complete\" or \"canonical\" name of the unit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:356
msgid "``Police Divisions`` are a class of police units in Nigeria established by law. There are over 1000 units of this type nationwide. However, each individual ``Police Division`` may not have a citation for their full, formal name. For example, a citation references the ``Achalla Division`` police unit in Anambra State. There is no citation for the fuller, more formal ``Achalla Police Division``. In this case, and similar cases, the Monitor would create a claim with ``Achalla Police Division`` in the :ref:`name:annotation <unit-name-annotation>` with citation ``50a65841-b429-4e77-b7e4-ac937dd2ae72``. This citation was created to specifically note the name for the unit has been created by the Monitor. Best practice is to also include any citations that provide contextual information for the standardized name, as well as a :ref:`public_notes:meta <unit-public-notes-meta>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:358
msgid "As many units do have standardized names, the Monitor follows several \"stylistic\" naming conventions for the :ref:`name:annotation <unit-name-annotation>` field:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:360
msgid "Numerals in Unit Names"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:362
msgid "The Monitor always standardizes the :ref:`name:annotation <unit-name-annotation>` to include numerals rather than spelling out numerals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:364
msgid "There may be a unit of which we only have citations for a ``Fourth Battalion``. In this case, the Monitor would create a claim with the :ref:`name:annotation <unit-name-annotation>` for the unit as ``4 Battalion`` using citation ``50a65841-b429-4e77-b7e4-ac937dd2ae72``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:366
msgid "In an effort to standardize names across all countries, the Monitor generally uses Arabic numerals in the :ref:`name:annotation <unit-name-annotation>` field. Where warranted by citations the Monitor will use Roman numerals like ``V`` or ``XI`` instead of ``5`` or ``11`` respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:368
msgid "Numbers Before Words in Unit Names"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:370
msgid "The Monitor lists the numeral in any :ref:`name:annotation <unit-name-annotation>` before any textual element of the name of the unit. This is done because the number of the unit is often the most distinguishing feature of a name."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:372
msgid "Most English language sources for Myanmar Army units reference the name of the unit and then the number, for example ``Light Infantry Battalion 510``. If there was no citation for ``510 Light Infantry Battalion`` the Monitor would create a claim with ``510 Light Infantry Battalion`` in the :ref:`name:annotation <unit-name-annotation>` with citation ``50a65841-b429-4e77-b7e4-ac937dd2ae72``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:375
msgid "Ordinal Indicators"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:377
msgid "The Monitor does not use ordinal indicators like ``1st`` or ``3rd`` in the :ref:`name:annotation <unit-name-annotation>` for units. Instead these will be listed in the :ref:`unit:names:assertion` field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:380
msgid "Language of Unit Names"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:382
msgid "The Monitor uses the name in the official or most commonly used language of the country where possible. Generally this is based on the Monitor's capacity at the time of the particular research project. A unit in the Mexican Army would be called by its name in Spanish (``10 Regimiento de Caballería Motorizado``), rather than the English translation ( ``10 Motorized Cavalry Regiment``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:386
msgid "Distinguishing Units with Same Name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:388
msgid "In cases where multiple units have the same name the Monitor will distinguish them by adding unique identifying text in parentheses based on the unit's location or parent unit. For example, there are multiple \"Central Police Station\" formations across Nigeria, some based in the same state. To better distinguish that these are separate, distinct units the Monitor added information on where the units were located to the name field for instance ``Central Police Station (Awka, Anambra State)``. Another example is in Myanmar where there are multiple different units all called \"1 Tactical Operations Command\". To distinguish them the Monitor added the parent unit inside of parentheses for each tactical operations command, such as ``1 Tactical Operations Command (1 Military Operations Command)``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:391
msgid "In some cases, we are aware that a unit exists because of what sources tell us about the general organizational structure. However, in some cases sources do not provide us with sufficient information to give these units a name, or to be precise about the nature of relationships between units. To resolve issues of this nature we use the concepts of \"Unnamed\" and \"Unknown\" units. We have written more about this in the Handbook page :ref:`Unknown and unnamed units`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:395
msgid "unit:names:assertion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:400
msgid "Any name given for a unit from citation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:420
msgid "``Deuxième RM``, ``GFSN``, ``IB-148``, ``IB (148)``, ``Infantry Battalion 148``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:425
msgid "Any name for a unit used in the citation should be entered in this field. While the :ref:`name:annotation <unit-name-annotation>` field is only used for a single, most complex value, this field is used for any name a citation uses for a unit. Thus, this field serves to capture \"aliases\" of a unit, which also includes any typos or misspellings from the citation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:427
msgid "Ordinal indicators, such as \"2nd\" or \"10/o\", should be entered in this field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:431
msgid "unit:classifications:assertion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:436
msgid "A general descriptor for the unit, often matches a branch or branches of the unit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:441 ../../source/units-identity.rst:492 ../../source/units-identity.rst:833
msgid "Text, controlled vocabulary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:456
msgid "``Army``, ``Ejército``, ``Police``, ``Military``, ``Military Police``, ``Joint Operation``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:462
msgid "We use classifications to describe the basic nature of a specific unit and to assist investigations of potential linkages between reports of human rights abuses and the Security Force Monitor's dataset. As alleged perpetrators are usually identified in general terms of \"soldiers\" and \"police\" this field is important as a first step to understand potential linkages between a :ref:`unit`, :ref:`person` and :ref:`incident`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:464
msgid "The :ref:`unit:classifications:assertion` field will contain a mix of standard terms and country-specific terms used to describe security force branches. In choosing terms to include in the :ref:`unit:classifications:assertion` field we try to include terms that are used by country experts as well as those that are common terms that would be meaningful to a general audience. We also try to be economical and create as few, distinct terms as possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:466
msgid "Units may have more than one classification. Usually this will be when a unit can have both \"generic\" and \"specific\" classifications."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:470
msgid "Units which are part of the army of a country may be coded as having a classification of ``Army`` as well as a classification of ``Military``, whereas units which are part of the navy of a country would have classifications of ``Navy`` and ``Military``. For both the army and navy unit their respective classifications are correct, the army and the navy are part of the military. Critically, this enables the Monitor or users of the Monitor's data to properly analyze allegations against \"soldiers\" and \"members of the army\" in the country. If the allegations are made against \"soldiers\" then the analysis should include every unit with the classification of ``Military``. If the allegations are against \"members of the army\" then only those units with a classification of ``Army`` would be included in the analysis. Other units which are part of the ``Military``, such as naval units with classifications of ``Navy`` and ``Military``, would be excluded, since they do not have a classification of ``Army``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:472
msgid "Every unit which has a \"specific\" classification should also have the \"generic\" classification as well."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:476
msgid "Units that have an ``Army`` classification should also have a :ref:`unit:classifications:assertion` of ``Military``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/units-identity.rst:478
msgid "Generally, classifications are \"taken on by\" superior units or the :ref:`relation:related_unit:refs:assertion` units, meaning that a :ref:`unit` should have all of the :ref:`unit:classifications:assertion` of any subordinate :ref:`unit`. For example, the :ref:`unit` representing the head of state or head of government which acts as commander-in-chief of the armed forces would have a :ref:`unit:classifications:assertion` of ``Military``, among others. See the section :ref:`Superior Units Taking On Positionings and Classifications of Subordinates` for more guidance."
msgstr ""

